# Measured seconds are per FILE, from docs/probes/ci-durations-4core.txt — the
# 4-core public runner, run 32753487844. The two older tables in docs/probes/
# were taken on the 2-core box a PRIVATE repository gets, and cutting from them
# is what left this leg wrong: on 2 cores the leg order was editor 151.3 > views
# 138.9 > graph 131.2 > rest 128.8 > coedit 125.6, and on 4 cores it is rest
# 120.4 > views 119.6 > coedit 109.1 > editor 99.6 > graph 89.7. The Chrome-heavy
# legs gained most from the extra cores and the plain-Python one gained least, so
# the cut did not scale down — it inverted.
#
# The cost model has a second term the old cut did not have, and `rest` is why it
# exists: 1103 tests carried 16s of collection and sub-50ms work that no duration
# row can see, against 3s for a leg of 79. So a leg is charged its attributed
# seconds PLUS ~2.5s fixed PLUS ~12ms per test, fitted on the five legs of that
# run. Cutting on attributed seconds alone underestimates exactly the leg that
# holds two thirds of the suite.
